<p>An index can store an ordered value, the keys inside a value, or a summary covering many values. Those choices determine which questions it can answer. A customer ID has an order that a B-tree can search. “Contains the tag gift” asks about an element inside an array. “Overlaps this reservation” asks about two intervals. “Falls on this day” can sometimes exclude whole ranges of table pages.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL supplies six built-in <em>access methods</em>: B-tree, hash, GIN, GiST, SP-GiST and BRIN. The method supplies the structure. An <em>operator class</em> connects a particular type and its operators to that structure. Choosing both starts with what the query means, then asks what information the index must retain to help answer it.</p>
<p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/indexes/">Indexes and access paths</a> develops composite keys, selectivity and ordered limits. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/architecture/">Inside PostgreSQL</a> follows an index entry to a visible heap tuple. Here we keep those ideas and change the information in the index. Small drawings are models; the plan observations come from an owned disposable PostgreSQL 18.6 server.</p>

<h2 id="methods-and-classes">Access methods and operator classes</h2>
<p><code>USING</code> selects the method. The name after an indexed column, when present, selects its operator class. Leaving it out chooses the default class for that type and method:</p>
<Sql label="Select a method and an operator class" code={`CREATE INDEX products_name_prefix
ON products USING btree (name text_pattern_ops);

CREATE INDEX product_tags_gin
ON product_tags USING gin (tags array_ops);`} />
<p>The first definition keeps a B-tree but changes how text is compared. The second indexes the component values of a <code>text[]</code> column. <code>product_tags</code> is a separate illustrative catalogue projection; P7 retains its established gift and ceramic tags. Neither definition changes the meaning of the SQL operator.</p>
<p>Figure 1 gives each method one question. Read the middle of each card: it is the information a search has available before it reaches the heap.</p>
<figure class="method-figure" aria-labelledby="method-map-title">
